-- title:
--   Counting C-fixed finite subgroups: #Y=p^{dim_Kspan_K Y}
-- statement:
--   Let $K \subseteq F$ be fields, $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $M$ be an additive commutative group carrying an $F$-module structure and a $K$-module structure which form a scalar tower over the algebra map. Let $p$ be a prime, and assume $K$ has characteristic $p$ and is perfect. Let $C \colon M \to M$ be an additive map which is $p^{-1}$-semilinear over $F$ in the sense that $C(f^p \cdot m) = f \cdot C(m)$ for all $f \in F$ and $m \in M$. Let $Y$ be an additive subgroup of $M$ which is finite, and suppose every element of $Y$ is fixed by $C$, i.e. $C(y) = y$ for all $y \in Y$. Then the cardinality of $Y$ is exactly
--   $$\#Y = p^{\,\dim_K \operatorname{span}_K(Y)},$$
--   where $\operatorname{span}_K(Y)$ is the $K$-submodule of $M$ generated by the underlying set of $Y$ and $\dim_K$ is its $K$-rank. No independence hypothesis on $Y$ is imposed: the equality, not merely an inequality, is asserted.
--
--   This is the counting form of the Lang–Katz disjointness property for the fixed points of a $p^{-1}$-semilinear (Cartier-type) operator: a finite group of $C$-fixed vectors is an $\mathbf{F}_p$-vector space whose $\mathbf{F}_p$-dimension coincides with the $K$-dimension of its span. It is used in the construction of the additive map from torsion into the differentials on a modular curve, where the group of Cartier-fixed elements must be counted against the dimension of the $K$-space it spans.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AddSubgroup_natCard_eq_pow_finrank_span_of_forall_apply_eq_self_of_map_pow_smul.lean

